A||N||4||Ronnie||electric window motor||||6||
Z||000000||Ronnie||11-05-2000||06:36 PM||||Can anyone tell me the best way to replace the electric window motor.Mine is shot!Already have a replacement , just want to know what the proper procedure would be.||||reg||6
Z||000001||fiero56||11-05-2000||09:11 PM||||Remove door handle(3 large phillips screws), remove inner door release shroud, remove inner door panel(held on by "clips"). Remove paper moisture barrier, unplug motor, drill out rivets, remove motor, install new motor using 5/16(?)self tapping bolts. Are you replacing the only the motor, or the motor and regulator(big gear thing)?||||reg||1
Z||000002||pollock||11-06-2000||10:58 AM||||Careful.<P>If memory serves, the fiero power window regulators depend on the motor to contain spring tension.  <P>What does this mean?  if you just remove the motor, the torsion spring in the regulator will unwind, causing some violent action on your workbench or garage floor.  Serious potential for injury.  Also, you'll never get that spring wound back up all the way.<P>Make sure that you secure the assembly in a vise before removing the motor.  ||||reg||4
Z||000003||Ronnie||11-06-2000||05:19 PM||||Thanks guys. I have the regulator and motor as a unit, pulled from a salvage yard.But, it was still a booger to get out of the door as a whole unit. I don't want to do my door like that one was treated.Do I need to pull the outer doorskin off to make it more accessible?||||reg||1
Z||000004||fiero56||11-06-2000||05:47 PM||||I would just leave the outer panel on. When I installed power windows in my Fiero, it took a few minutes to figure it out. Use a c-clamp to hold the window in the full up posistion, slide in the regulator/motor, and align the 3 wheels in the tracks. Then bolt it in, and your set. Michigan Fiero site has a pretty good write up.||||reg||1
